#B3. Discover 3 proactive security implementations in practice.

1. Shift-left security
Integrate auto code analyzers and vulnerability scanners into a CI/CD (continuous integration and continuous deployment )software pipeline.
Reasons:
Security shifts left directly into the development cycle instead of after software runtime.
  While a developer commits code:
  SAST (static application security testing)-parses the source code for logical vulnerabilities
  SCA (software composition analysis)-scans open-source depenedcies for vulnerabilities and exposures
  DAST (dynamic application security testing)-checking live deployment issues by attack a temporary container. The pipline will automatically reject the build before the malicious code enter the server.

2. Honeytokens and canary tokens
Cyeber deception environment, applying fake assets of production networks, such as dummy API keys in code repository configuration or simulated database.
Reasons:
Traditional mechanisms has been known that create a lot of noise by log analysis. Deception technology is able to catch attacker before they can pinpoint users property during the early scannin phase.
It triggers a silent alert once the attacker engaged in network reconnaissance or attempted to read and execute one of those fake keys.
Also, to apply this technology has to make sure that a legit user has absolutely no functional reason to access a honeytoken.

3. CASM (continuous attack surface management)
It maps out digital footprint from an outside-in perspective by deploying automated external tools.
Reasons:
Traditional scanning is usually periodic, like monthly or quarterly, 
however, CASM operates 24/7 to dicover 'mistakes', such as forgotten testing domains, orphaned cloud storage buckets or unpatch legacy portals, which are accidentally exposed to the internet.
By dicovering these 'mistakes', this allows defenders to pull them offline before getting attack by malicious script bots scan the public IP blocks.
